window.MOD={"n": 2, "title": "The Skeleton for Yoga Teachers", "hours": 2, "tagline": "Bones are the frame every pose is built on, and the landmarks you can see are how a teacher reads that frame in a room.", "overview": "This module gives you a working map of the skeleton: not every one of the 206 bones, but the major ones you will name, feel and cue in every class. You will learn the difference between the axial skeleton, the protective central column of skull, spine and ribs, and the appendicular skeleton of limbs and girdles. Then you will follow the body from the skull down the spine, out through the shoulder to the weight-bearing hand, and down through the pelvis to the foot as the foundation of standing poses.\n\nThe final lesson turns anatomy into observation. Six bony landmarks, the ASIS, iliac crest, greater trochanter, acromion, C7 and malleoli, let you see alignment from across the room and help students find it in their own bodies. Throughout, we stay honest about what bones can and cannot do: bone shape differs between people, so range differs too, and a teacher's job is to adapt and refer, never to force or diagnose.", "objectives": ["By the end of this module you can classify the major bones as axial or appendicular and describe five functions of bone.", "By the end of this module you can name the regions of the spine, their vertebra counts and their curves, and explain why twists come mainly from the thoracic spine.", "By the end of this module you can identify the bones of the shoulder girdle, arm and hand, and give options for students with wrist discomfort in weight-bearing poses.", "By the end of this module you can describe the pelvis, femur, lower leg and foot, including the foot tripod, and explain why hip range varies between people.", "By the end of this module you can locate the ASIS, iliac crest, greater trochanter, acromion, C7 and malleoli on yourself and use them in clear, non-forcing cues."], "lessons": [{"id": "2.1", "title": "Axial and appendicular skeleton: what bones do", "mins": 20, "slides": [{"type": "stat", "value": "206", "label": "bones in the adult skeleton", "body": "About 80 form the axial skeleton down the midline. The other 126 form the appendicular skeleton: the limbs and the girdles that attach them.", "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m2-skeleton", "caption": "Axial skeleton in amber, appendicular in green"}, "notes": "Let's start with the big picture. An adult skeleton has 206 bones, and a newborn has more, closer to 270, because many bones fuse as we grow. You do not need to memorise all 206 to teach yoga. What helps is one simple split. The axial skeleton is the central axis of the body: skull, spine, ribs and breastbone. Think of it as the pole of a tent. The appendicular skeleton is everything that hangs off that pole: the arms and legs, plus the two girdles that connect them, the shoulder girdle and the pelvic girdle. On the diagram, the axial bones are amber and the appendicular bones are green. Notice that the hip bones are green even though they feel central. Hold that thought for lesson four."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Two halves of one skeleton", "columns": [{"head": "Axial (about 80)", "points": ["Skull, spine, ribs, sternum", "Protects brain, cord, heart, lungs", "Built mainly for stability", "Home of breath and posture"]}, {"head": "Appendicular (about 126)", "points": ["Shoulder and pelvic girdles", "Arms, hands, legs, feet", "Built mainly for movement", "Reaches, grips, steps, balances"]}], "notes": "Here is the contrast in one view. The axial skeleton is mostly about protection and stability. The skull guards the brain, the spine guards the spinal cord, and the ribs guard the heart and lungs while still moving with every breath. The appendicular skeleton is mostly about movement and contact with the world: the hands that grip the mat and the feet that root into it. Of course nothing is purely one thing. The spine moves a great deal, and the legs are very stable when you stand. But the split gives you a quick map. When a student says their back hurts in a twist, you are in the axial world. When they say their wrist hurts in plank, you are in the appendicular world."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "What bones actually do", "points": ["Support: a frame to stand and hold poses", "Protect: skull, spine and ribs guard vital organs", "Move: bones are levers that muscles pull on", "Store and make: minerals, fat and blood cells"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["trunk", "thighL", "thighR", "shinL", "shinR"], "joints": [], "caption": "Mountain: bones stacked to carry load with little effort"}, "notes": "Bones do more than hold us up. First, support: in Mountain Pose your bones stack so that muscles only need to make small corrections. Second, protection: the skull, spinal column and ribcage wrap the most delicate organs. Third, movement: bones act as levers. Muscles pull on them across joints, and that is every pose you will ever teach. Fourth, storage and production. Bone holds most of the body's calcium and phosphate and releases them into the blood when needed. The red marrow inside some bones, such as the hip bones, sternum and vertebrae, makes red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets. Yellow marrow stores fat. So a skeleton is a working organ system, not a coat hanger."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Bone is living tissue that answers to load", "points": ["Bone cells constantly break down and rebuild bone", "Regular load tends to keep bone stronger", "Yoga's effect on bone density is still uncertain", "Osteoporosis: adapt poses, avoid forceful loaded flexion"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "chaturanga", "highlight": ["armL", "armR", "foreL", "foreR"], "joints": ["shoulderL", "shoulderR", "elbowL", "elbowR"], "caption": "Weight-bearing on the arms loads the arm bones"}, "notes": "Bone looks like stone, but it is alive. Cells called osteoclasts break old bone down and osteoblasts lay new bone. This remodelling responds to the forces bone meets, an idea known as Wolff's law. Load a bone regularly and it tends to maintain or build density. Remove load, as in long bed rest or spaceflight, and bone thins. Poses like Plank and Chaturanga put real load through the arm bones, and standing poses load the legs. Be honest with students, though. Some small studies suggest yoga may help bone density, but the evidence is limited and mixed, so do not promise that yoga reverses osteoporosis. If a student tells you they have osteoporosis, avoid forceful, weighted spinal rounding and deep loaded twists, keep balance work safe, and encourage them to follow their doctor's guidance."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: axial and appendicular in Tree", "steps": ["Stand in Mountain. Tap your skull, breastbone and ribs: that is your axial core.", "Shift into Tree. Notice the standing leg, a stack of appendicular bones, carrying you.", "Raise your arms. Feel the shoulder blades glide on the ribs as the girdle moves.", "Breathe in. Feel the axial ribcage widen while the limbs stay steady."], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "tree", "highlight": ["trunk", "neck"], "joints": ["hipL", "hipR"], "caption": "Tree: an axial column balanced on one leg"}, "notes": "Let's feel the two halves. Stand in Mountain and tap the top of your head, then your breastbone, then run your fingers along a few ribs. That is your axial skeleton. Now shift your weight into one foot and come into Tree. Your standing leg, from the hip bone to the foot, is appendicular, and right now it is holding the whole axial column up. Raise your arms overhead and notice how your shoulder blades slide over the back of the ribs. The shoulder girdle is appendicular, so it moves on the axial ribcage rather than being welded to it. Finally, take a slow breath and feel the ribcage widen. Even the protective axial skeleton moves with every breath."}, {"type": "check", "question": "Which bone belongs to the appendicular skeleton?", "options": ["Sternum", "Scapula", "Sacrum"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The scapula is part of the shoulder girdle, which connects the arm to the axial skeleton.", "notes": "Take a moment before the answer. The sternum is the breastbone in the centre of the chest, so it is axial. The sacrum is the base of the spine, so it is axial too, even though it sits inside the pelvis. The scapula, or shoulder blade, is part of the shoulder girdle, and the girdles belong to the appendicular skeleton along with the limbs. A good rule: if it is part of the skull, spine, ribs or sternum, it is axial. If it is a limb or a girdle, it is appendicular."}], "guide": {"brief": "The adult skeleton has 206 bones. For teaching, the most useful split is between the axial skeleton and the appendicular skeleton. The axial skeleton, about 80 bones, runs down the midline: the skull, the vertebral column, the ribs and the sternum (breastbone). It protects the brain, spinal cord, heart and lungs, and it is the framework for posture and breathing. The appendicular skeleton, about 126 bones, is everything that hangs from that axis: the shoulder girdle (clavicles and scapulae), the arms and hands, the pelvic girdle (the two hip bones), and the legs and feet.\n\nBones have several jobs. They support the body, protect organs, and act as levers for muscles, which is how every pose happens. They also store calcium and phosphate, store fat in yellow marrow, and make blood cells in red marrow, found in places like the hip bones, sternum and vertebrae. Bones come in different shapes that suit their jobs: long bones such as the femur, short bones such as the wrist bones, flat bones such as the sternum and skull plates, irregular bones such as the vertebrae, and sesamoid bones such as the kneecap, which sits inside a tendon.\n\nBone is living tissue. It is constantly broken down and rebuilt, and it adapts to the loads it meets. Regular weight-bearing tends to help maintain bone, and lack of load weakens it. Yoga includes a lot of weight-bearing through both arms and legs, but research on yoga and bone density is limited and mixed, so teachers should not claim that yoga reverses osteoporosis. For students with known osteoporosis, teachers adapt: avoid forceful loaded spinal flexion and deep weighted twists, make balance safe, and defer to the student's medical team.", "key_points": ["206 adult bones: about 80 axial and 126 appendicular.", "Axial = skull, spine, ribs, sternum. Appendicular = limbs plus shoulder and pelvic girdles.", "Bones support, protect, act as levers, store minerals and fat, and make blood cells.", "Bone remodels throughout life and responds to load.", "Evidence that yoga builds bone is limited: adapt for osteoporosis and refer, never promise a cure."], "feel_it": "Stand in Mountain and tap your skull, breastbone and a few ribs to find your axial skeleton. Move into Tree and notice that the standing leg, an appendicular stack, carries the whole axial column. Raise your arms and feel your shoulder blades glide over the back of your ribs. Take three slow breaths and feel the ribcage widen and narrow.", "cue_it": ["Let your bones stack so your muscles can soften a little.", "Feel the standing leg carry the weight of your spine and head.", "As you breathe in, let the ribs widen in every direction."]}}, {"id": "2.2", "title": "Skull, spine, ribs and sternum", "mins": 25, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "The skull balances on the top of the spine", "points": ["Cranium protects the brain; facial bones frame the face", "Occipital bone rests on C1, the atlas", "The head weighs roughly 4.5 to 5 kg", "C1 and C2 allow much of head turning"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["neck"], "joints": [], "caption": "Mountain: the head balanced over the spine"}, "notes": "The skull has two main parts. The cranium is the rounded case around the brain, made of flat bones joined by fixed joints called sutures. The facial bones form the face, and the mandible, or jawbone, is the only skull bone that moves freely, at the temporomandibular joint just in front of the ear. At the back of the skull, the occipital bone has two smooth bumps that rest on the first cervical vertebra, called the atlas, much like the Titan who held up the sky. The nodding yes movement happens largely there. The second vertebra, the axis, has a peg that the atlas pivots around, and about half of the side to side no movement happens at this pair. An average adult head weighs roughly 4.5 to 5 kilograms, so where it sits over the spine matters in every pose."}, {"type": "stat", "value": "7 / 12 / 5", "label": "cervical, thoracic and lumbar vertebrae", "body": "Below the lumbar spine sit the sacrum, five fused vertebrae, and the coccyx, three to five small fused bones. Seen from the side, the column makes four gentle curves.", "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m2-spine", "caption": "Side view of the vertebral column: regions and curves"}, "notes": "The vertebral column has 24 separate vertebrae plus two fused sections. There are 7 cervical vertebrae in the neck, 12 thoracic vertebrae in the upper and middle back, and 5 lumbar vertebrae in the low back. A simple memory hook is breakfast at 7, lunch at 12 and dinner at 5. Below those is the sacrum, five vertebrae fused into one triangular bone, and the coccyx or tailbone. Look at the side view. The neck and low back curve forward, called lordosis. The thoracic spine and the sacrum curve backward, called kyphosis. These curves act a little like a spring, sharing load. Each vertebra has a solid body at the front that carries weight, separated from the next by an intervertebral disc, and a bony arch at the back that protects the spinal cord."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Each region moves in its own way", "columns": [{"head": "Cervical", "points": ["Small, very mobile vertebrae", "Nods, turns and tilts the head", "Keep heavy load off it"]}, {"head": "Thoracic", "points": ["Ribs attach here", "Rotates well, extends less", "Most of a twist lives here"]}, {"head": "Lumbar", "points": ["Largest, strongest vertebrae", "Bends forward and back easily", "Little rotation per segment"]}], "notes": "Every region of the spine has vertebrae shaped for its job. The cervical spine is the most mobile. It lets us nod, turn and tilt the head, which is why we avoid putting heavy body weight on it, as in an unsupported headstand for a beginner. The thoracic spine carries the ribs. The ribs make it stiffer for bending back, but the shape of its joints allows good rotation. The lumbar vertebrae are the largest because they carry the most weight. Their joints are shaped to allow bending forward and backward, but they rotate only a few degrees each. This has a direct teaching message: in a seated twist, most of the turn should come from the thoracic spine, the neck and the hips, not from cranking the low back."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Ribs and sternum: a cage that moves", "points": ["12 pairs of ribs join the thoracic vertebrae", "Pairs 1 to 7 attach directly to the sternum", "Pairs 8 to 10 join by shared cartilage; 11 and 12 float", "Cartilage lets the cage expand with each breath"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "cobra", "highlight": ["trunk"], "joints": [], "caption": "Cobra: the chest opens as the thoracic spine extends"}, "notes": "The thoracic cage is 12 pairs of ribs, the sternum and the thoracic vertebrae. Every rib joins a thoracic vertebra at the back. At the front, ribs 1 to 7 attach to the sternum through their own strip of cartilage, so they are called true ribs. Ribs 8 to 10 are false ribs because they join the cartilage of the rib above. Ribs 11 and 12 are floating ribs, with no front attachment. The sternum has three parts: the manubrium at the top, the body, and the small xiphoid process at the bottom. The flexible cartilage is what lets the ribcage lift and widen when we breathe in. In Cobra, the thoracic spine extends and the front of the chest broadens. The breathing module will build on this."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: find your curves and your ribs", "steps": ["Stand side on to a wall, heels, sacrum and upper back touching it.", "Slide a hand behind your low back and neck: feel the two forward curves.", "Place hands on your side ribs and breathe in slowly: feel them widen.", "Find the notch at the top of your sternum, then the xiphoid at the bottom."], "visual": {"kind": "figure", "angles": {"view": "side", "trunk": 90, "neck": 90, "uL": -82, "fL": -78, "uR": -82, "fR": -78, "tL": -90, "sL": -90, "ftL": 0, "tR": -90, "sR": -90, "ftR": 0}, "highlight": ["trunk", "neck"], "joints": ["pelvis"], "caption": "Mountain from the side: the spine's natural curves"}, "notes": "Let's find the curves in your own body. Stand with your back near a wall so your heels, the back of your pelvis and your upper back touch it. Now slide a hand into the space behind your low back. That space is your lumbar lordosis. There is a similar space behind your neck: cervical lordosis. Where your upper back touches the wall is the gentle rounding of thoracic kyphosis. Everyone's curves are a little different, and that is normal. Next, place your hands on the sides of your ribs and breathe in slowly. Feel them move out under your hands. Finally, find the dip at the top of your breastbone, the jugular notch, and walk your fingers down to the small tip at the bottom, the xiphoid process."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "Twist from the ribcage", "cue": "Lengthen up through the crown as you breathe in. As you breathe out, turn from the ribs and let the belly follow.", "why": "The thoracic spine is built to rotate while the lumbar spine allows only a few degrees per segment, so the turn should begin higher up.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["trunk"], "joints": ["pelvis"], "caption": "Rotation comes mainly from the thoracic spine"}, "notes": "Here is how the anatomy turns into words. In a twist we want the rotation spread along the spine, especially through the thoracic region, which is designed for it. The lumbar spine rotates only a little, so if a student forces a twist by pulling hard with the arms, the extra force tends to land on the low back and the sacroiliac area. The cue invites length first, because slumping reduces how well the thoracic spine can rotate. Then the turn begins at the ribcage and the belly follows. Notice what the cue does not say. It does not promise to wring out toxins or detox organs. Twists are good for spinal movement and they feel good. That is reason enough, and it is honest."}, {"type": "check", "question": "A student forces a deep seated twist by levering with the arms. Which region is least built for rotation?", "options": ["Cervical spine", "Thoracic spine", "Lumbar spine"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Lumbar vertebrae allow only a few degrees of rotation each, so forced twisting tends to load the low back.", "notes": "The answer is the lumbar spine. The cervical spine rotates a lot, especially at C1 and C2. The thoracic spine rotates well because of how its joints are angled. The lumbar spine is built for bending forward and back, and each segment rotates only slightly. So when you see a student pulling hard into a twist, cue them to lengthen, ease off the arms, and let the turn come from the ribcage. The arms are there to guide the twist, not to force it past where the spine wants to go."}], "guide": {"brief": "The skull sits on top of the axial skeleton. The cranium is a case of flat bones joined by fixed sutures, and the facial bones form the face. The mandible is the only freely moving skull bone. The occipital bone at the back of the skull rests on C1, the atlas, where much of nodding happens. C1 pivots on C2, the axis, and that pair provides about half of the head's rotation. The head weighs roughly 4.5 to 5 kg, which is why head position matters in every pose.\n\nThe vertebral column has 7 cervical, 12 thoracic and 5 lumbar vertebrae, then the sacrum (five fused) and the coccyx (three to five fused). Seen from the side it has four curves: cervical and lumbar lordosis (curving forward) and thoracic and sacral kyphosis (curving back). A typical vertebra has a weight-bearing body at the front, a disc between it and its neighbours, and an arch at the back protecting the spinal cord, with a spinous process you can feel as a bump under the skin. Each region moves differently. The cervical spine is the most mobile. The thoracic spine rotates well but its ribs limit backbending. The lumbar spine flexes and extends freely but rotates only a few degrees per segment, so twists should come mainly from the thoracic spine, neck and hips.\n\nThe thoracic cage is 12 pairs of ribs, the sternum and the thoracic vertebrae. Ribs 1 to 7 are true ribs attached to the sternum by their own cartilage, ribs 8 to 10 are false ribs joined through the cartilage above, and ribs 11 and 12 float. The sternum has a manubrium, a body and a xiphoid process. Costal cartilage lets the cage expand as we breathe. When teaching twists, be modest: they move the spine and feel good, but the popular claim that they detox the organs is not supported by evidence.", "key_points": ["Skull rests on C1 (atlas); C1 turns on C2 (axis).", "7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, then sacrum and coccyx.", "Four curves: cervical and lumbar lordosis, thoracic and sacral kyphosis.", "Twist mostly from the thoracic spine; the lumbar spine rotates little.", "12 rib pairs: 7 true, 3 false, 2 floating; cartilage allows breathing movement."], "feel_it": "Stand with your heels, pelvis and upper back against a wall. Slide a hand behind your low back and then behind your neck to feel the two forward curves. Place your hands on your side ribs and breathe in slowly to feel them widen. Finally, trace your sternum from the notch at the top down to the xiphoid tip.", "cue_it": ["Grow tall through the crown of the head, then turn from the ribs.", "Let the back of the neck stay long, as if the head floats on top of the spine.", "Breathe into the sides and back of your ribs."]}}, {"id": "2.3", "title": "Shoulder girdle and upper limb, down to the weight bearing hand", "mins": 25, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "The shoulder girdle floats on the ribcage", "points": ["Two bones per side: clavicle and scapula", "Only bony link to the trunk: the sternoclavicular joint", "Scapula glides on the back of the ribs", "Shallow socket: mobile, but depends on muscle"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior2", "highlight": ["armL", "armR"], "joints": ["shoulderL", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Warrior II: arms carried by the shoulder girdle"}, "notes": "The shoulder girdle is two bones on each side: the clavicle, or collarbone, in front and the scapula, or shoulder blade, behind. The clavicle joins the sternum at the sternoclavicular joint, and that small joint is the only bony connection between your whole arm and your trunk. Everything else is held by muscle. The scapula is not attached to the ribs by a joint at all; it glides over them, cushioned by muscle. At its outer corner is the glenoid fossa, a shallow socket for the head of the humerus. A shallow socket means huge mobility, which is why we can reach overhead in Warrior One. It also means the shoulder relies heavily on muscles for stability, so strength matters as much as flexibility for shoulders."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "From shoulder to wrist", "points": ["Humerus: the single upper arm bone", "Radius on the thumb side, ulna on the little finger side", "Radius turns around the ulna to rotate the palm", "Olecranon of the ulna is the point of the elbow"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "dog", "highlight": ["armR", "foreR"], "joints": ["shoulderR", "elbowR"], "caption": "Down Dog: humerus, radius and ulna in a line of load"}, "notes": "Below the shoulder is the humerus, the long bone of the upper arm. Its rounded head sits in the glenoid fossa. At the elbow it meets two forearm bones. The ulna is on the little finger side, and its bony tip, the olecranon, is the point of your elbow that you lean on. The radius is on the thumb side. When you turn your palm down, called pronation, the radius crosses over the ulna. When you turn it up, supination, they sit side by side. In Downward Dog the palms are pressed flat, so the forearms are pronated, while we often cue the upper arms to rotate outward. That pairing only works because the forearm and the upper arm can rotate separately."}, {"type": "stat", "value": "27", "label": "bones in each wrist and hand", "body": "8 carpals in two rows, 5 metacarpals in the palm and 14 phalanges in the fingers. The hand was built for grip and fine skill, not for carrying body weight.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "chaturanga", "highlight": ["foreL", "foreR"], "joints": ["elbowL", "elbowR"], "caption": "Chaturanga: the hands carry a large share of body weight"}, "notes": "Each wrist and hand contains 27 bones. The 8 carpal bones sit in two rows at the wrist. The 5 metacarpals form the palm, and the 14 phalanges make the fingers: three in each finger and two in the thumb. The carpals form an arch whose hollow side faces the palm, and that arch forms the floor of the carpal tunnel, through which tendons and the median nerve pass. Here is the key idea for yoga. The hand evolved for gripping and fine skill. In Plank, Chaturanga and Downward Dog we ask it to carry a large share of body weight with the wrist bent back close to its limit. Most people's wrists extend roughly 70 degrees, and Plank asks for nearly 90. No wonder wrist discomfort is a common complaint in vinyasa classes."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: map the weight-bearing hand", "steps": ["Come to tabletop, wrists under shoulders, fingers spread comfortably wide.", "Rock onto the heels of your hands, then onto your fingertips. Notice the difference.", "Press evenly through the knuckle at the base of each finger, especially index and thumb.", "Feel the centre of the palm lift slightly, like a gentle dome."], "visual": {"kind": "figure", "angles": {"view": "side", "trunk": 17, "neck": 10, "uL": -90, "fL": -90, "uR": -90, "fR": -90, "tL": -90, "sL": 180, "ftL": 180, "tR": -90, "sR": 180, "ftR": 180}, "highlight": ["armL", "armR", "foreL", "foreR"], "joints": ["shoulderR", "elbowR"], "caption": "Tabletop: learning to share weight across the hand"}, "notes": "Come onto hands and knees with your wrists roughly under your shoulders. Spread your fingers, but not so far that it strains. First, lean back so most of your weight falls into the heels of your hands, right over the wrist. Notice how that feels. Now lean forward into your fingertips. Then find the middle. Press down through the knuckle at the base of each finger, especially the base of the index finger and the thumb. You may feel the centre of your palm lift a little, like a dome. This is the same idea as the arch of the foot. By spreading the load across the knuckles and fingers, less of it sits in the wrist crease at the heel of the hand."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "Share the load through the hand", "cue": "Spread your fingers and press through the base of your index finger and thumb, so the wrist is not carrying you alone.", "why": "The wrist is extended near its end range in Plank and Down Dog; spreading load toward the knuckles and fingers reduces pressure on the heel of the hand.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "dog", "highlight": ["foreL", "foreR"], "joints": ["shoulderL", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Down Dog: rooting the hands"}, "notes": "This cue is one of the most useful you will teach. It asks the hand to work actively rather than collapse into the heel of the palm. Many teachers find it helps students with sensitive wrists, and it matches what we know about how the load sits on an extended wrist. It is, however, a practical cue rather than something proven by large trials, so keep other options ready. If a student still has wrist pain, offer a folded mat edge or wedge under the heels of the hands to reduce extension, let them come onto fists or onto the forearms, or shorten the time spent in weight-bearing. And if pain persists or there is numbness or tingling in the fingers, suggest they see a health professional rather than guessing at a cause."}, {"type": "check", "question": "Which is the only joint that connects the upper limb bones to the axial skeleton?", "options": ["Glenohumeral joint", "Sternoclavicular joint", "Acromioclavicular joint"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The clavicle meets the sternum at the sternoclavicular joint; everything else attaching the arm to the trunk is muscle.", "notes": "The answer is the sternoclavicular joint, where the clavicle meets the sternum. The glenohumeral joint is the ball and socket between the humerus and the scapula, and the acromioclavicular joint is where the clavicle meets the acromion of the scapula. Both of those are within the shoulder girdle and arm. This single small bony link is why the shoulder is so mobile, and why muscle strength is so important for keeping it stable in weight-bearing poses like Plank, Chaturanga and Side Plank. Flexibility alone does not protect a shoulder."}], "guide": {"brief": "The shoulder girdle is made of the clavicle and scapula on each side. The clavicle joins the sternum at the sternoclavicular joint, the only bony connection between the arm and the trunk. The scapula glides over the back of the ribs, held there by muscle. Its outer corner has the acromion, which forms the bony top of the shoulder, and the glenoid fossa, a shallow socket that receives the head of the humerus. The shallow socket gives the shoulder the largest range of motion in the body but leaves it dependent on muscles for stability.\n\nThe humerus is the upper arm bone. At the elbow it meets the ulna on the little finger side and the radius on the thumb side. The olecranon of the ulna is the point of the elbow. The radius rotates around the ulna to turn the palm down (pronation) or up (supination), which is why a student can keep the palms flat in Downward Dog while rotating the upper arms outward.\n\nEach wrist and hand has 27 bones: 8 carpals in two rows, 5 metacarpals and 14 phalanges. The carpals form an arch over the carpal tunnel, where finger tendons and the median nerve pass. The hand is designed for grip and skill, yet vinyasa asks it to bear weight with the wrist extended close to its end range, roughly 90 degrees in Plank compared with a typical range of about 70 degrees. Teachers can help by cueing students to spread the fingers and press through the knuckles, especially the base of the index finger and thumb, so load is shared across the whole hand. Offer options such as a wedge under the heels of the hands, fists, or forearms, and refer on if pain, numbness or tingling persists.", "key_points": ["Shoulder girdle = clavicle + scapula; only bony link to the trunk is the sternoclavicular joint.", "Shallow glenoid socket: very mobile, needs muscular support.", "Humerus above; radius (thumb side) and ulna (little finger side) below.", "27 bones per wrist and hand: 8 carpals, 5 metacarpals, 14 phalanges.", "Weight-bearing hands: spread load through knuckles and fingers, offer wrist options."], "feel_it": "Come to tabletop with your wrists roughly under your shoulders. Rock your weight into the heels of your hands, then into your fingertips, and notice how different they feel. Settle in the middle and press through the base of each finger, especially the index finger and thumb. Feel the centre of the palm lift slightly, like the arch of a foot.", "cue_it": ["Press the base of your index finger and thumb down into the mat.", "Let your shoulder blades glide wide across your back.", "If your wrists complain, come onto your fists or forearms."]}}, {"id": "2.4", "title": "Pelvis and lower limb, down to the foot as foundation", "mins": 25, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "The pelvis: two hip bones and the sacrum", "points": ["Each hip bone is ilium, ischium and pubis fused", "They meet the sacrum at the sacroiliac joints", "In front they meet at the pubic symphysis", "The acetabulum is the deep hip socket"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m2-pelvis", "caption": "Pelvis from the front, with the key landmarks"}, "notes": "The bony pelvis is a ring. On each side is a hip bone, and at the back sits the sacrum, wedged between them. Each hip bone begins as three separate bones that fuse during the teenage years. The ilium is the broad upper wing, whose top edge is the iliac crest where your hands rest on your hips. The ischium is the lower back part, and its thick bottom edge, the ischial tuberosity, is your sitting bone. The pubis is at the front. The two hip bones meet at the front at the pubic symphysis, a joint with a pad of cartilage. At the back each hip bone meets the sacrum at a sacroiliac joint, which is very strong and moves only a few degrees. Where the three parts meet is the acetabulum, the cup-shaped socket for the head of the femur."}, {"type": "compare", "title": "Shoulder girdle and pelvic girdle", "columns": [{"head": "Shoulder girdle", "points": ["Light, open, floats on ribs", "Shallow socket for the humerus", "Built for reach and range"]}, {"head": "Pelvic girdle", "points": ["Heavy ring joined to the sacrum", "Deep socket for the femur", "Built for carrying weight"]}], "notes": "Comparing the two girdles explains a great deal about practice. The shoulder girdle is light and open at the back, it floats on the ribcage, and its socket is shallow. It trades stability for range. The pelvic girdle is a heavy, closed ring firmly joined to the spine through the sacrum, and its socket, the acetabulum, is deep and cups much of the femoral head. It trades range for stability, because it carries the whole upper body. This is why the hips generally feel steadier in standing poses, and why shoulders need more active muscular support in arm balances. It also reminds us that when a student lacks hip range, the limit may be the shape of the bones, not only tight muscles."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "The femur: longest bone, angled neck", "points": ["Femoral head sits deep in the acetabulum", "Neck angles out to the greater trochanter", "Shaft slopes inward toward the knee", "Bone shapes vary, so hip range varies"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior2", "highlight": ["thighL", "thighR"], "joints": ["hipL", "hipR"], "caption": "Warrior II: the femurs rotate outward in the hip sockets"}, "notes": "The femur is the longest and strongest bone in the body. Its ball-shaped head sits in the acetabulum. Below the head is the neck, which angles outward at about 125 degrees in an average adult, leading to a big bony knob at the side, the greater trochanter. The shaft then slopes inward so that the knees sit closer together than the hips. Here is the part many trainings miss. The angle of the femoral neck, its forward twist, and the depth and direction of the socket all vary from person to person. Two students with equally flexible muscles can reach very different depths in Warrior Two or Lotus because their bones meet at different points. So we teach the pose to the student's body, and we never force a joint past where bone meets bone."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Knee and lower leg: tibia carries, fibula steadies", "points": ["Femur meets tibia at the knee; patella glides in front", "Tibia carries most of the body's weight", "Fibula is slender, mainly for muscle attachment", "Malleoli: tibia inside, fibula outside the ankle"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "chair", "highlight": ["shinL", "shinR", "thighL", "thighR"], "joints": ["kneeL", "kneeR"], "caption": "Chair: knees track over the feet"}, "notes": "At the knee, the rounded ends of the femur, called condyles, rest on the flat top of the tibia, with cartilage pads called menisci between them. The patella, or kneecap, is a sesamoid bone that sits inside the quadriceps tendon and glides in a groove on the front of the femur, giving the thigh muscles better leverage. Below the knee, the tibia or shinbone carries most of the body's weight. The fibula is thin and sits on the outside. It carries only a small share of load and mainly anchors muscles. At the ankle, the lower end of the tibia forms the inner ankle bone, the medial malleolus, and the lower end of the fibula forms the outer ankle bone, the lateral malleolus. In Chair Pose we ask the knees to track in line with the feet so load passes through the tibia evenly."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: build the foot tripod", "steps": ["Stand in Mountain and lift all ten toes.", "Feel three points: big toe mound, little toe mound and centre of the heel.", "Keep those points grounded as you lower the toes softly.", "Notice the inner arch gently lift without gripping the toes."], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m2-foot", "caption": "Foot bones, the medial arch and the three-point tripod"}, "notes": "Each foot has 26 bones: 7 tarsals including the talus and the heel bone or calcaneus, 5 metatarsals and 14 phalanges. They form three arches: the long inner or medial arch, the long outer or lateral arch, and the transverse arch across the middle of the foot. Let's feel them. Stand in Mountain and lift all your toes. Notice weight settling into three points: the mound under the big toe, the mound under the little toe and the centre of the heel. That is the foot tripod. Keep those three points down and lower the toes softly. You may feel the inner arch lift slightly. Arch height varies a lot between healthy people, and a low arch is not automatically a problem, so we invite this rather than correct feet."}, {"type": "check", "question": "Which bone forms the outer ankle bone, the lateral malleolus?", "options": ["Tibia", "Fibula", "Talus"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The lateral malleolus is the lower end of the fibula; the medial malleolus is the lower end of the tibia.", "notes": "The answer is the fibula. The lower end of the fibula forms the outer ankle bone, which sits a little lower and further back than the inner one. The tibia forms the inner ankle bone, the medial malleolus. The talus sits between the two malleoli like a block in a slot, and has no muscles attached to it at all. Knowing these bumps helps in Tree Pose, where we place the foot above or below the knee, and in any pose where you want to check that weight is spread evenly across the ankle."}], "guide": {"brief": "The bony pelvis is a ring made of two hip bones and the sacrum, with the coccyx below. Each hip bone forms from three bones that fuse in the teenage years: the ilium (the broad wing, topped by the iliac crest and ending in front at the ASIS), the ischium (whose ischial tuberosity is the sitting bone) and the pubis (which meets its partner at the pubic symphysis). At the back, each hip bone joins the sacrum at a strong sacroiliac joint that moves only a few degrees. The three parts meet at the acetabulum, the deep socket for the femoral head. The hip bones form the pelvic girdle and are appendicular, while the sacrum and coccyx belong to the axial spine.\n\nCompared with the shoulder girdle, the pelvic girdle is heavier, closed and firmly joined to the spine, with a deep socket. It is built for stability and weight bearing rather than range. The femur is the longest, strongest bone. Its head sits in the acetabulum, its neck angles outward to the greater trochanter, and its shaft slopes inward to the knee. The angles of the femoral neck and the socket vary between people, so hip range in poses like Warrior II, Pigeon or Lotus differs partly because of bone shape, not only muscle length. Teachers should never force a hip past its end point.\n\nAt the knee the femur rests on the tibia, and the patella, a sesamoid bone inside the quadriceps tendon, glides in front. The tibia carries most of the body's weight; the slender fibula mainly anchors muscles. Their lower ends form the medial and lateral malleoli. Each foot has 26 bones: 7 tarsals, 5 metatarsals and 14 phalanges, arranged in medial, lateral and transverse arches. A helpful teaching model is the foot tripod: the big toe mound, little toe mound and heel. Arch height varies widely in healthy people, so teachers invite even grounding rather than diagnosing flat feet.", "key_points": ["Hip bone = ilium + ischium + pubis, fused; pelvis = two hip bones + sacrum (+ coccyx).", "Acetabulum is a deep socket: the hip trades range for stability.", "Femur: head, angled neck, greater trochanter; bone shape varies between people.", "Tibia carries weight; fibula anchors muscles; malleoli are their lower ends.", "26 bones per foot, three arches, and a three-point tripod for grounding."], "feel_it": "Stand in Mountain and lift all ten toes. Notice the weight settle into three points: the big toe mound, the little toe mound and the centre of the heel. Keep those points grounded as you lower the toes softly, and notice whether the inner arch lifts slightly. Rock gently forward and back to find the place where all three points share the load.", "cue_it": ["Root down through the big toe mound, the little toe mound and the heel.", "Let your knees point the same way as your second and third toes.", "Sit down onto your sitting bones and let the spine rise up from them."]}}, {"id": "2.5", "title": "Landmarks you can see and cue: ASIS, iliac crest, greater trochanter, acromion, C7, malleoli", "mins": 25, "slides": [{"type": "concept", "title": "Landmarks are bones you can see and feel", "points": ["Palpable bony points lie just under the skin", "They let you read alignment from across the room", "Students can find them on themselves", "Use them for reference, not to force symmetry"], "visual": {"kind": "diagram", "id": "m2-landmarks", "caption": "Six key landmarks from the front and back"}, "notes": "A landmark is a point of bone close enough to the surface that you can see it or feel it. For a teacher they are gold, because they turn invisible anatomy into something you can observe from across the room and something students can find with their own hands. This lesson covers six: the ASIS at the front of the pelvis, the iliac crest along the top of the pelvis, the greater trochanter at the side of the hip, the acromion at the top of the shoulder, the C7 vertebra at the base of the neck and the malleoli at the ankle. We will add the PSIS dimples at the back. One caution before we begin. Landmarks help you see, but real bodies are not perfectly symmetrical, and small differences are normal."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "ASIS: the front hip points", "cue": "Place your hands on your front hip points and let them turn toward the front of the mat, as far as your back hip comfortably allows.", "why": "The ASIS is the forward tip of the iliac crest; watching the two points shows how the pelvis is turned, and many hips cannot square fully in Warrior I.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior1", "highlight": ["trunk"], "joints": ["pelvis", "hipL", "hipR"], "caption": "Warrior I: the ASIS points show where the pelvis faces"}, "notes": "The anterior superior iliac spine, or ASIS, is the bony point at the front of each hip, where the iliac crest ends. Most people can feel it easily when they put their hands on their hips and slide them forward. In Warrior One, looking at the two ASIS points tells you which way the pelvis is facing. The old cue was square the hips to the front. Many students cannot do that comfortably, because the back hip would have to extend and turn inward further than its bones and tissues allow, and the strain then shifts to the knee or low back. So the modern cue is toward the front, as far as comfortable. A shorter, wider stance also helps. The landmark is a guide for noticing, not a target to force."}, {"type": "stat", "value": "L4", "label": "level of a line across the tops of the iliac crests", "body": "Hands on hips rest on the iliac crests. A line joining their highest points crosses the spine at about the fourth lumbar vertebra.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": ["core"], "joints": ["pelvis"], "caption": "Mountain: iliac crests level at the waist"}, "notes": "The iliac crest is the long curved top of the ilium. When students put their hands on their hips, their hands rest on it. From behind, you can often see whether the two crests look level. If one looks higher, that may reflect how the student is standing, a leg length difference, or simply normal asymmetry. It is something to notice, never something to diagnose. Clinicians use a useful fact: a line joining the tops of the two crests crosses the spine at about the fourth lumbar vertebra. That helps you locate the low back. Just below the back of the crests are two small dimples, the posterior superior iliac spines or PSIS, which mark roughly where the sacroiliac joints are."}, {"type": "cue", "title": "Acromion and C7: shoulders and neck", "cue": "Let the tops of your shoulders widen away from your ears, and keep the bump at the base of your neck soft and long.", "why": "The acromion is the bony top of the shoulder; C7 has the most prominent spinous process at the base of the neck, so both show how the shoulders and head are carried.", "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "warrior2", "highlight": ["neck", "armL", "armR"], "joints": ["shoulderL", "shoulderR"], "caption": "Warrior II: acromions level and away from the ears"}, "notes": "The acromion is the flat bony shelf at the very top of the shoulder, part of the scapula. Walk your fingers out along the collarbone to the edge of the shoulder and you will find it. In Warrior Two, looking at the acromions tells you whether the shoulders are hiked toward the ears or evenly spread. C7 is the seventh cervical vertebra. Its spinous process is usually the most prominent bump at the base of the neck. A handy trick: place a finger on the biggest bump and look up. If it slides away under your finger, it was C6, and C7 is the next one down. In forward folds or in Cobra, a long, soft area around C7 is a sign the neck is not being cranked back."}, {"type": "concept", "title": "Greater trochanter and malleoli", "points": ["Greater trochanter: bony bump on the outer hip", "It moves when the thigh rotates", "Medial malleolus (tibia) sits higher than lateral (fibula)", "In Tree, place the foot above or below the knee"], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "tree", "highlight": ["thighL", "thighR"], "joints": ["hipL", "hipR", "kneeL", "kneeR", "ankleL", "ankleR"], "caption": "Tree: hips, knee and ankle as reference points"}, "notes": "The greater trochanter is the big bump on the outside of the upper thigh, about a hand's width below the iliac crest. Put your hand on it and turn your foot in and out, and you will feel it roll back and forth, because it is part of the femur. It is a good way for students to feel hip rotation in Warrior Two or Triangle. At the ankle, the medial malleolus is the inner ankle bone, the end of the tibia, and the lateral malleolus is the outer ankle bone, the end of the fibula. The outer one sits lower and further back. In Tree Pose, we invite the lifted foot to press above or below the knee rather than on the side of the knee joint, which is not built to be pushed sideways."}, {"type": "steps", "title": "Feel it: a landmark tour on yourself", "steps": ["Hands on hips: find the iliac crests, then slide forward to the ASIS points.", "Thumbs back: feel for the PSIS dimples beside the sacrum.", "Find the greater trochanter and turn the foot in and out to feel it move.", "Trace the collarbone out to the acromion; find C7, then both malleoli."], "visual": {"kind": "pose", "pose": "mountain", "highlight": [], "joints": ["pelvis", "hipL", "hipR", "shoulderL", "shoulderR", "ankleL", "ankleR"], "caption": "Mountain: a full landmark tour"}, "notes": "Let's take a tour of your own landmarks. Stand in Mountain and put your hands on your hips. That is the iliac crest. Slide your fingers forward and down to the front hip points: the ASIS. Now slide your thumbs back to the low back and feel for two small dimples on either side of the sacrum: the PSIS. Next, drop a hand to the side of your upper thigh, find the big bony bump, and turn your foot in and out. That is the greater trochanter moving. Then trace a collarbone outward to the flat shelf at the top of the shoulder, the acromion. Find the big bump at the base of your neck, C7. Finally, reach down and touch the inner and outer ankle bones."}, {"type": "check", "question": "In Warrior I a student's back hip will not turn to face forward. What is the best response?", "options": ["Press on the ASIS until the hips square", "Invite the hips toward the front as far as comfortable, and offer a shorter, wider stance", "Tell them their pelvis is misaligned"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Bone shape and back hip range limit how square the pelvis can get, so invite and offer options rather than force or diagnose.", "notes": "The answer is to invite, not force. Pushing on someone's pelvis without consent is never acceptable, and even with consent, forcing a pelvis to square can shift strain into the knee, sacroiliac joint or low back. Telling a student their pelvis is misaligned is a diagnosis, which is outside our scope, and it may cause needless worry. A shorter, wider stance gives the back hip more room. Using the ASIS as a reference simply helps the student notice where they are."}], "guide": {"brief": "Bony landmarks are points of bone near the surface that you can see or feel. They make anatomy visible in a class and give students something concrete to find on themselves. Six are especially useful. The ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) is the front hip point at the forward end of the iliac crest. It shows which way the pelvis faces, for example in Warrior I, where many students cannot square the hips fully because of bone shape and limited back hip extension. The iliac crest is the curved top of the pelvis where hands rest on hips; a line across the tops of both crests crosses the spine at about L4. Just below the back of the crests are the PSIS dimples beside the sacrum.\n\nThe greater trochanter is the bony bump on the outer upper thigh, part of the femur. It rolls forward and back as the thigh rotates, which helps students feel hip rotation in Warrior II or Triangle. The acromion is the flat bony shelf at the top of the shoulder, at the outer end of the scapula; it shows whether the shoulders are lifted toward the ears. C7 usually has the most prominent spinous process at the base of the neck. If the bump you touch slides forward when you look up, it is C6, and C7 is the next one down. The medial malleolus (end of the tibia) is the inner ankle bone and the lateral malleolus (end of the fibula) is the outer one, sitting lower and further back. In Tree, the lifted foot presses above or below the knee, not on it.\n\nUse landmarks to observe and to cue self-discovery. Bodies are rarely perfectly symmetrical, so an uneven crest or shoulder is something to notice, not to diagnose. Invite students to touch their own landmarks, and ask for clear consent before any hands-on assist. When something looks unusual or a student reports pain, adapt the pose and suggest they speak to a health professional.", "key_points": ["ASIS = front hip points; they show where the pelvis faces.", "Iliac crests = hands-on-hips line, level with about L4; PSIS dimples sit below at the back.", "Greater trochanter rolls as the thigh rotates.", "Acromion = top of the shoulder; C7 = biggest bump at the base of the neck.", "Medial malleolus (tibia) is higher; lateral malleolus (fibula) is lower. Observe, never diagnose."], "feel_it": "Stand in Mountain with your hands on your hips to find the iliac crests, then slide forward to the ASIS points and back to the PSIS dimples. Place a hand on the greater trochanter and turn your foot in and out to feel it roll. Trace your collarbone out to the acromion, find the biggest bump at the base of your neck, and look up to check it is C7. Finish by touching your inner and outer ankle bones.", "cue_it": ["Bring your front hip points toward the front of the mat, only as far as feels good.", "Let the tops of your shoulders slide away from your ears.", "Press your foot into your inner thigh or calf, above or below the knee."]}}], "terms": [["Axial skeleton", "The central column of about 80 bones: skull, spine, ribs and sternum. In Mountain it is the stacked core the limbs hang from."], ["Appendicular skeleton", "The 126 bones of the limbs and the shoulder and pelvic girdles. In Tree the standing leg is appendicular."], ["Vertebra", "One of the bones of the spine, with a weight-bearing body in front and a protective arch behind. You feel their spinous processes as bumps when rolling down."], ["Lordosis", "A forward curve of the spine, found in the neck and low back. It is the hollow you feel behind your low back in Mountain against a wall."], ["Kyphosis", "A backward curve of the spine, found in the thoracic and sacral regions. It is the gentle rounding of the upper back that touches the wall."], ["Intervertebral disc", "A pad of cartilage between two vertebral bodies with a tough outer ring and a gel-like centre. It shares load as you bend in Forward Fold."], ["Atlas and axis (C1, C2)", "The top two cervical vertebrae. The skull nods on the atlas and turns as the atlas pivots on the axis, as when you look over your shoulder in Warrior II."], ["Sternum", "The breastbone, made of the manubrium, body and xiphoid process. It lifts gently as the chest opens in Cobra."], ["Clavicle", "The collarbone, joining the sternum to the scapula. It lifts slightly when you reach the arms overhead in Warrior I."], ["Scapula", "The shoulder blade, which glides over the back of the ribs. It slides wide across the back in Plank."], ["Radius and ulna", "The two forearm bones: radius on the thumb side, ulna on the little finger side. The radius crosses the ulna when the palms press flat in Down Dog."], ["Carpals", "The eight small bones of the wrist in two rows. They carry weight through the heel of the hand in Plank."], ["Ilium, ischium and pubis", "The three bones that fuse to form each hip bone. You sit on the ischium's sitting bones in Easy Pose."], ["Acetabulum", "The deep cup-shaped hip socket that holds the head of the femur. The femur rotates within it in Warrior II."], ["Femur", "The thighbone, the longest and strongest bone. Its angled neck and head shape help set how far a student can go in Lotus."], ["Patella", "The kneecap, a sesamoid bone inside the quadriceps tendon. It lifts as you engage the thighs in Mountain."], ["Tibia and fibula", "The lower leg bones: the tibia carries most of the weight, the slender fibula mostly anchors muscles. The tibia stacks over the ankle in Chair."], ["Foot tripod", "The three grounding points of the foot: big toe mound, little toe mound and heel. Rooting all three steadies Tree."], ["ASIS", "Anterior superior iliac spine: the front hip point at the end of the iliac crest. Its direction shows where the pelvis faces in Warrior I."], ["Greater trochanter", "The bony bump on the outer upper thigh, part of the femur. It rolls back as the thigh turns out in Warrior II."], ["Acromion", "The flat bony shelf at the top of the shoulder, part of the scapula. Cue it away from the ears in Warrior II."], ["Malleoli", "The ankle bones: medial (inner, from the tibia) and lateral (outer, from the fibula). In Tree the foot rests above or below the knee, not on these or the knee joint."]], "misconceptions": [["Bones are dead, fixed structures.", "Bone is living tissue that is constantly broken down and rebuilt and responds to the loads placed on it."], ["Yoga reverses osteoporosis.", "Weight-bearing helps bone in general, but evidence that yoga improves bone density is limited and mixed. Adapt poses and follow the student's medical advice."], ["Everyone can square the hips in Warrior I with enough effort.", "Hip range depends partly on bone shape and back hip extension. Invite the hips toward the front as far as comfortable instead of forcing."], ["Twists wring out toxins from the organs.", "There is no good evidence for detox by twisting. Twists move the spine, mostly at the thoracic level, and can simply feel good."], ["Flat feet or an uneven pelvis mean something is wrong.", "Arch height and minor asymmetry vary widely in healthy people. Teachers observe and adapt; they do not diagnose."]], "flashcards": [["How many bones are in the adult skeleton?", "206: about 80 axial and 126 appendicular. Mountain Pose stacks them all over the feet."], ["What makes up the axial skeleton?", "The skull, vertebral column, ribs and sternum. It is the central column you lengthen in Mountain."], ["What makes up the appendicular skeleton?", "The limbs plus the shoulder and pelvic girdles. The arms in Warrior II and the standing leg in Tree are appendicular."], ["Name five functions of bone.", "Support, protection, leverage for movement, mineral and fat storage, and blood cell production. Every pose uses the leverage function."], ["What is Wolff's law in plain words?", "Bone adapts to the loads it meets, tending to strengthen with regular load. Plank and standing poses load bone."], ["How many vertebrae are in each region?", "7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, then the fused sacrum and coccyx. Rolling down to Forward Fold moves through them all."], ["Which spinal curves are lordotic?", "The cervical and lumbar curves, which bend forward. Feel the lumbar hollow when standing against a wall."], ["Which spinal curves are kyphotic?", "The thoracic and sacral curves, which bend backward. The upper back touches the wall in Mountain."], ["Which vertebrae turn the head the most?", "C1 (atlas) pivoting on C2 (axis) gives about half of head rotation, as when you turn to gaze over the hand in Warrior II."], ["Why should twists come mainly from the thoracic spine?", "The lumbar spine rotates only a few degrees per segment, while the thoracic spine rotates well. Cue the turn from the ribs."], ["What are true, false and floating ribs?", "Ribs 1 to 7 attach directly to the sternum, 8 to 10 join via cartilage above, 11 and 12 float. All move with breath."], ["What are the three parts of the sternum?", "Manubrium, body and xiphoid process. The sternum lifts as the chest opens in Cobra."], ["What bones form the shoulder girdle?", "The clavicle and scapula on each side. The scapulae glide wide in Plank."], ["What is the only bony joint linking arm to trunk?", "The sternoclavicular joint. Everything else is muscle, so strength matters in arm balances."], ["Which forearm bone is on the thumb side?", "The radius. It crosses the ulna when the palms press flat in Down Dog."], ["How many bones are in the wrist and hand?", "27: 8 carpals, 5 metacarpals and 14 phalanges. They share the load in Plank when you press through the knuckles."], ["Why do wrists often complain in vinyasa?", "Plank asks for near 90 degrees of wrist extension, beyond many people's range. Offer wedges, fists or forearms."], ["What three bones fuse to form each hip bone?", "Ilium, ischium and pubis, meeting at the acetabulum. You sit on the ischium in seated poses."], ["Where are the sacroiliac joints?", "Where the sacrum meets each ilium at the back of the pelvis. The PSIS dimples mark them roughly."], ["Why is the hip more stable than the shoulder?", "The acetabulum is deep and the pelvic ring is joined to the spine. The hip trades range for stability in standing poses."], ["Why can two students reach different depths in Lotus?", "Femoral neck angle and hip socket shape vary, so bone can limit range regardless of flexibility."], ["Which lower leg bone carries most weight?", "The tibia. In Chair we stack the knee over the foot so load passes through it evenly."], ["How many bones are in each foot?", "26: 7 tarsals, 5 metatarsals and 14 phalanges. They form the arches you root through in Mountain."], ["What is the foot tripod?", "The big toe mound, little toe mound and heel. Grounding all three steadies balance in Tree."], ["Where is the ASIS?", "The front hip point at the forward end of the iliac crest. It shows where the pelvis faces in Warrior I."], ["How do you confirm you have found C7?", "Touch the largest bump at the base of the neck and look up. If it slides away, it was C6; C7 is below."], ["Which malleolus sits lower?", "The lateral malleolus, the end of the fibula. In Tree the foot goes above or below the knee, not on it."], ["How can a student feel the greater trochanter move?", "Place a hand on the outer hip bump and turn the foot in and out. It rolls as the thigh rotates in Warrior II."]], "exercises": [{"type": "mcq", "q": "A student asks whether their hip bones are part of the spine. Which answer is accurate?", "options": ["Yes, the hip bones are axial", "No, the hip bones are appendicular, but the sacrum between them is axial", "No, the whole pelvis is appendicular, including the sacrum", "Yes, the pelvis is a fused part of the lumbar spine"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The two hip bones form the pelvic girdle (appendicular), while the sacrum and coccyx belong to the vertebral column (axial)."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "A student with osteoporosis joins your class. Which choice best fits the evidence and your scope?", "options": ["Tell them yoga will rebuild their bones", "Include loaded full forward folds to strengthen the spine", "Avoid forceful loaded spinal flexion, keep balance safe, and follow their doctor's guidance", "Refuse to teach them"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Evidence for yoga improving bone density is limited, and loaded flexion can be risky, so adapt and defer to their medical team."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "In a seated twist, a student hooks an elbow and pulls hard. Where is the forced rotation most likely to strain?", "options": ["The cervical spine", "The thoracic spine", "The lumbar spine and sacroiliac region", "The shoulder girdle only"], "answer": 2, "explain": "The lumbar spine rotates only a few degrees per segment, so extra force tends to land on the low back and SI region."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "Your student feels wrist pain in Plank. Which suggestion is the best first option?", "options": ["Lock the elbows and sink into the heels of the hands", "Spread the fingers, press through the knuckles, and try a wedge under the heels of the hands", "Push through the pain to strengthen the wrist", "Diagnose carpal tunnel syndrome"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Sharing load across the hand and reducing wrist extension are practical first options; persistent pain or tingling should be referred."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "Two equally flexible students reach very different depths in Warrior II. What is a likely reason?", "options": ["One is not trying hard enough", "Differences in femoral neck angle and hip socket shape", "One has a shorter spine", "The patella blocks hip rotation"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Bone shape at the hip varies between people and can set the end of range regardless of muscle flexibility."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "In Tree, where should the lifted foot be placed?", "options": ["Directly on the side of the standing knee", "Above or below the standing knee", "On the lateral malleolus", "Anywhere, it makes no difference"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The knee joint is not built to be pushed sideways, so the foot rests on the inner thigh or calf instead."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "You want to find a student's low back level from behind. Which landmark line helps?", "options": ["A line across the acromions, level with T1", "A line across the tops of the iliac crests, level with about L4", "A line across the malleoli, level with L5", "A line across the greater trochanters, level with T12"], "answer": 1, "explain": "The line joining the tops of the iliac crests crosses the spine at about L4."}, {"type": "mcq", "q": "A student touches the biggest bump at the base of their neck and looks up; the bump slides forward and disappears. What did they find?", "options": ["C7", "C6", "T1", "The acromion"], "answer": 1, "explain": "C6 moves forward in neck extension, while C7 stays more prominent, so the next bump down is C7."}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each landmark to where you find it", "pairs": [["ASIS", "Front hip point"], ["Iliac crest", "Where hands rest on the hips"], ["Greater trochanter", "Bump on the outer upper thigh"], ["Acromion", "Flat bony top of the shoulder"], ["C7", "Largest bump at the base of the neck"], ["Lateral malleolus", "Outer ankle bone"]]}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each bone to its region", "pairs": [["Atlas", "Top of the cervical spine"], ["Xiphoid process", "Lower tip of the sternum"], ["Olecranon", "Point of the elbow"], ["Ischial tuberosity", "Sitting bone"], ["Calcaneus", "Heel"]]}, {"type": "match", "title": "Match each count to what it counts", "pairs": [["206", "Bones in the adult skeleton"], ["12", "Thoracic vertebrae or pairs of ribs"], ["5", "Lumbar vertebrae"], ["27", "Bones in one wrist and hand"], ["26", "Bones in one foot"]]}, {"type": "sort", "title": "Sort each bone into the axial or appendicular skeleton", "buckets": ["Axial", "Appendicular"], "items": [["Sternum", "Axial"], ["Sacrum", "Axial"], ["Ribs", "Axial"], ["Skull", "Axial"], ["Clavicle", "Appendicular"], ["Hip bone", "Appendicular"], ["Femur", "Appendicular"], ["Scapula", "Appendicular"], ["Carpals", "Appendicular"]]}, {"type": "sort", "title": "Sort each feature into the girdle it describes", "buckets": ["Shoulder girdle", "Pelvic girdle"], "items": [["Shallow socket", "Shoulder girdle"], ["Floats on the ribcage", "Shoulder girdle"], ["Built for reach and range", "Shoulder girdle"], ["Deep socket", "Pelvic girdle"], ["Joined to the sacrum", "Pelvic girdle"], ["Built to carry body weight", "Pelvic girdle"], ["Includes the acromion", "Shoulder girdle"], ["Includes the ASIS", "Pelvic girdle"]]}, {"type": "order", "title": "Put the regions of the vertebral column in order from top to bottom", "items": ["Cervical", "Thoracic", "Lumbar", "Sacrum", "Coccyx"]}, {"type": "order", "title": "Trace the bones of the upper limb from the trunk to the palm", "items": ["Clavicle", "Scapula", "Humerus", "Radius and ulna", "Carpals", "Metacarpals"]}], "self_check": [["What is the difference between the axial and appendicular skeleton?", "The axial skeleton is the central column of skull, spine, ribs and sternum, about 80 bones. The appendicular skeleton is the limbs and the shoulder and pelvic girdles, about 126 bones."], ["Name the four spinal curves and whether each is lordotic or kyphotic.", "Cervical and lumbar curves are lordotic, curving forward. Thoracic and sacral curves are kyphotic, curving backward."], ["Why should a twist come mainly from the thoracic spine?", "Thoracic joints allow good rotation while the lumbar spine rotates only a few degrees per segment. Forcing a twist loads the low back."], ["Why is the shoulder more mobile but less stable than the hip?", "The glenoid socket is shallow and the shoulder girdle floats on the ribs, attached by bone only at the sternoclavicular joint. The hip has a deep socket in a pelvic ring joined to the spine."], ["Give two options for a student with wrist pain in Downward Dog.", "Cue them to spread the fingers and press through the knuckles, and offer a wedge under the heels of the hands, fists or forearms. Refer on if pain or tingling persists."], ["What is the foot tripod and why is it useful?", "The big toe mound, little toe mound and heel. Grounding all three spreads load and steadies balance poses like Tree."], ["How would you use the ASIS in Warrior I without forcing?", "Invite the front hip points toward the front of the mat only as far as comfortable, and offer a shorter, wider stance if the back hip cannot turn."], ["Which bones form the medial and lateral malleoli, and which sits lower?", "The medial malleolus is the end of the tibia and the lateral malleolus is the end of the fibula. The lateral one sits lower."]]};
